  "statement": "Given an integer array nums, return _the number of **AND triples**_.\n\nAn **AND triple** is a triple of indices `(i, j, k)` such that:\n\n*   `0 <= i < nums.length`\n*   `0 <= j < nums.length`\n*   `0 <= k < nums.length`\n*   `nums[i] & nums[j] & nums[k] == 0`, where `&` represents the bitwise-AND operator.\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n```\nInput: nums = [2,1,3]\nOutput: 12\nExplanation: We could choose the following i, j, k triples:\n(i=0, j=0, k=1) : 2 & 2 & 1\n(i=0, j=1, k=0) : 2 & 1 & 2\n(i=0, j=1, k=1) : 2 & 1 & 1\n(i=0, j=1, k=2) : 2 & 1 & 3\n(i=0, j=2, k=1) : 2 & 3 & 1\n(i=1, j=0, k=0) : 1 & 2 & 2\n(i=1, j=0, k=1) : 1 & 2 & 1\n(i=1, j=0, k=2) : 1 & 2 & 3\n(i=1, j=1, k=0) : 1 & 1 & 2\n(i=1, j=2, k=0) : 1 & 3 & 2\n(i=2, j=0, k=1) : 3 & 2 & 1\n(i=2, j=1, k=0) : 3 & 1 & 2\n```\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n```\nInput: nums = [0,0,0]\nOutput: 27\n```\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n*   `1 <= nums.length <= 1000`\n*   `0 <= nums[i] < 2^(16)`",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= nums.length <= 1000",
    "0 <= nums[i] < 216"
  ],
  "examples": [
